{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Primary scalable candidate: replace dense companion-matrix powering with exact Kitamasa polynomial exponentiation. Compute x^(H-1) modulo the recurrence characteristic polynomial, then combine its coefficients with A1..AN in O(N^2 log H); use dynamic O(N) storage. Independently verify ordering/indexing on N=1, H=N+1, zeros, and values near the modulus.\",\"B\":\"Develop an independent generating-function/Bostan-Mori solution. Construct the correct numerator from the base terms and denominator 1-P1*x-...-PN*x^N, then use parity halving with exact modular polynomial products. Avoid approximate transforms; retain this candidate only if its complete-program cost beats the simpler recurrence method.\",\"C\":\"Own a low-latency hybrid candidate. Use a rolling direct recurrence when H-N is genuinely small and a cache-friendly quadratic exponentiation path otherwise, with dispatch based only on input dimensions/work estimates. Eliminate fixed dense matrices and the source's unnecessary dense final matrix product; investigate guarded, race-free four-core polynomial kernels only where their overhead is repaid.\",\"G\":\"Treat A, B, and C as competing complete programs, not automatically additive patches. Validate each and any merge against broad oracle-style cases, especially coefficient orientation, modulo arithmetic, N=1, and boundary exponents. Measure repeated end-to-end runs on the ARM64 target against the valid 0.020203 s baseline; select the fastest correct whole candidate (including baseline if necessary) and only retain integration changes with independent evidence.\",\"analysis\":\"The valid baseline is very short (mean 0.020203 s, four correctness cases), so startup and parallel overhead may dominate measured inputs. Its source nevertheless performs full dense n-by-n companion matrix products and squarings, O(N^3 log H), plus a dense matrix-by-vector-in-disguise final product; this becomes tens of billions of operations near N=1020. Exact linear-recurrence methods provide the main scalable opportunity, while C explores generic low-gap and systems-cost cases. All candidates must preserve the input/output semantics for every valid input and must not hard-code expected results.\"}"}}
